From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 12:13:25 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: t8132-j604: Disable the AOP audio group
 for now

The device tree on this branch enables three blocks for the first time
on the 14-inch MacBook Pro (M4, 2024): PCIe port 0 with its DART, the
two USB-C ports with their SPMI port controllers, and the AOP audio
group. With it the machine hangs early in boot: the boot logo stays up
and nothing is printed, both with this kernel and with an older one
(MEASURED 2026-11-04). The same device tree with these three blocks
disabled, and nothing else changed, boots with this kernel (MEASURED
2026-11-04).

Which of the three blocks causes the hang, alone or only in
combination, is not known yet (root cause TO-VERIFY). Disable the AOP,
its DART and mailbox, both ADMACs, i2c1 with the CS42L84 headphone
codec, and the headphone jack card, which has no other consumer, until
a J604 boot with them enabled has been shown to work. Only status
lines are changed or added, so enabling them again is a revert of this
change.

/chosen still asks for the AOP's DAPF handoff. m1n1 hands the AOP over
only when its node is enabled, so the request has no effect while the
AOP is disabled, and keeping it means that enabling the group again
needs nothing beyond the revert.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts | 20 ++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
index 9b27f48a60..d06121462a 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
@@ -64,6 +64,7 @@
 		model = "MacBook Pro J604 Jack";
 		#address-cells = <1>;
 		#size-cells = <0>;
+		status = "disabled"; /* with the AOP, see &aop */
 
 		dai-link@0 {
 			reg = <0>;
@@ -607,9 +608,16 @@
  * memory-region when /chosen asks for the AOP's handoff. Until m1n1 does so
  * for the J604, the AOP driver finds no memory-region and fails its probe
  * before touching the AOP, and the cards below never bind.
+ *
+ * Disabled for now, with its DART, mailbox, both ADMACs, i2c1 and the jack
+ * card: a J604 device tree that enables them together with PCIe and the
+ * USB-C ports hangs early boot, before the console. While the AOP node is
+ * disabled m1n1 does not hand the AOP over, so the /chosen request above is
+ * inert. Enable them again once a J604 boot with them enabled has been
+ * shown to work.
  */
 &aop {
-	status = "okay"; /* DTV-AUD-1, DTV-AUD-2, DTV-AUD-13 */
+	status = "disabled"; /* DTV-AUD-1, DTV-AUD-2, DTV-AUD-13 */
 };
 
 &aop_audio {
@@ -621,19 +629,19 @@
 };
 
 &aop_dart {
-	status = "okay"; /* DTV-AUD-4 */
+	status = "disabled"; /* DTV-AUD-4 */
 };
 
 &aop_mbox {
-	status = "okay"; /* DTV-AUD-3 */
+	status = "disabled"; /* DTV-AUD-3 */
 };
 
 &admac0 {
-	status = "okay"; /* DTV-AUD-5 */
+	status = "disabled"; /* DTV-AUD-5 */
 };
 
 &admac1 {
-	status = "okay"; /* DTV-AUD-6 */
+	status = "disabled"; /* DTV-AUD-6 */
 };
 
 /*
@@ -642,7 +650,7 @@
  * is AP GPIO 98, level low. The AOP firmware runs the jack's stream.
  */
 &i2c1 {
-	status = "okay";
+	status = "disabled"; /* with the AOP, see &aop */
 
 	jack_codec: codec@4b {
 		compatible = "cirrus,cs42l84";
